# Reflux - thirty patches: the classics first, and then what this machine
# has that they did not.
#
# Thirteen parameters, and eight of them are one idea: a resonant filter with
# an envelope thrown at it. So the first half of this bank is a tour of that
# one idea - how far open, how much envelope, how fast it shuts, how far a
# note slides into the next - because that is what a bank of this kind is
# judged on, and anyone reaching for this machine is reaching for it first.
#
# The second half is the four controls that are not part of that idea: the
# sub oscillator, the pulse width, the band-pass mode and the drive. None of
# them existed on the instrument this descends from, and they are why the
# machine has its own name rather than being a tribute act.
#
# **Levelled to -21 dB on the harness's loudness column**, which is the house
# figure across every machine in the app rather than a number chosen for this
# bank alone. Getting there took fourteen decibels out of the machine's
# output rather than out of each patch's volume knob (`kHouse` in
# Reflux.cpp): every machine here had been levelled against its own bank and
# none against the others, and the factory had come to span twenty-five
# decibels, so changing machine changed the volume of the song. Done in the
# machine, the knob still means the same thing wherever you are and Init
# lands with the rest of the bank instead of fourteen decibels above it.
#
# Five patches sit below the house figure and all five are the band-passed
# ones, which take both ends off a spectrum and most of its energy with them:
# two to six decibels down, all of them already at the top of their volume.
# That is what the mode costs and not something to pretend away. Drive does
# not buy it back - its own compensation is slightly over-generous, so
# leaning on it measures quieter.
#
# **A fixed band on a moving note runs out.** Reed and Glass are band-passed
# and their bands do not follow the keyboard, so below a certain note the
# band sits twenty-five or thirty-five harmonics above the fundamental and
# there is no note left in it. Their ranges start where the band can still
# reach, which is a real property of the mode rather than a fault.
#
# **The demo is sixteenths with rests, slides and accents** (`role acid`),
# because what this machine does happens between the notes rather than on
# them. The two bars of eighths it used to be showed that a bass machine
# worked and showed nothing about this one.
#
# **The sub oscillator can take the note off you.** Grind was written at 0.8
# and the octave below sat eighteen decibels ahead of the played note, so you
# pressed one key and heard another; it is at 0.4. Sub Drop is the patch
# where that is the point, and it stops at note 45 because above there the
# sub has nothing left to be an octave below.
#
# Every patch says the notes it is played in, so the app puts the keyboard
# there when it is loaded. Every note of every range now reads within twenty
# cents and none is on the wrong partial.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Reflux` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes.

machine Reflux
# Sixteenths with rests, slides and accents, because what this machine is
# for happens between the notes rather than on them. Eighths showed that a
# bass machine worked and showed nothing about this one.
role    acid

patch Init family=classic

# --- The classics ----------------------------------------------------
#
# What the machine everybody means does, and what a bank of this kind is
# judged on first.

patch Classic family=classic  note=36 range=24..55
  # The one everybody means. Open enough to hear the note, resonant enough to
  # hear the filter, and a decay that shuts before the next eighth.
  #
  # Further from the machine's own defaults than it first was: at 520 Hz and
  # 0.62 it measured the same as Init, and a bank whose first two entries are
  # the same sound has nine patches in it, not ten.
  cutoff         410 Hz
  resonance      0.72
  envmod         0.64
  decay          280 ms
  accent         0.75
  slide          50 ms
  drive          0.16
  volume         0.88

patch Squelch family=classic  note=36 range=24..55
  # Further into the resonance and a much faster decay: the blip rather than
  # the sweep. Accent nearly all the way up, because on a line like this the
  # accented steps are the line.
  cutoff         330 Hz
  resonance      0.9
  envmod         0.85
  decay          130 ms
  accent         0.92
  slide          40 ms
  drive          0.22
  volume         0.90

patch Rubber family=classic  note=36 range=24..55
  # A pulse instead of a saw, wide slide, and enough drive to round the peak
  # off. The one that bends between notes rather than stepping.
  wave           #1
  pw             0.42
  cutoff         295 Hz
  resonance      0.75
  envmod         0.7
  decay          150 ms
  accent         0.8
  slide          130 ms
  drive          0.25
  volume         0.74

patch "Long Sweep" family=motion  note=36 range=24..55
  # A decay far longer than a step, so the filter never closes and the line
  # rises across the bar instead of inside each note.
  cutoff         180 Hz
  resonance      0.72
  envmod         0.95
  decay          1600 ms
  accent         0.45
  slide          60 ms
  drive          0.15
  volume         0.91

patch "Slide Line" family=motion  note=36 range=24..55
  # Every note glides into the next. The slide is the other half of what
  # this machine is for and nothing else here is built around it: at half a
  # second the line stops being notes and becomes one moving pitch.
  cutoff         380 Hz
  resonance      0.68
  envmod         0.6
  decay          260 ms
  accent         0.7
  slide          420 ms
  drive          0.14
  volume         0.89

patch Doorstop family=short  note=36 range=24..55
  # Shut. Almost no cutoff, almost all envelope, and a decay short enough
  # that each note is a thud with a pitch somewhere inside it.
  cutoff         90 Hz
  resonance      0.55
  envmod         1
  decay          90 ms
  accent         0.85
  slide          25 ms
  drive          0.3
  volume         0.98

patch Round family=classic  note=36 range=24..55
  # No resonance at all and no envelope: the note, warm, with the filter just
  # taking the top off. Every bank needs the one that is simply a bass, and
  # a bank of nothing but acid does not have it.
  cutoff         520 Hz
  resonance      0.0
  envmod         0.0
  decay          300 ms
  accent         0.35
  slide          45 ms
  drive          0.05
  volume         0.99

patch "Open Saw" family=classic  note=36 range=24..55
  # The filter out of the way, so you can hear what it is doing to
  # everything else in the bank. Raw oscillator, drive off, and the only
  # thing shaping the note is its own decay.
  cutoff         9000 Hz
  resonance      0.05
  envmod         0.0
  decay          500 ms
  accent         0.3
  drive          0.0
  volume         0.87

patch Tick family=short  note=36 range=24..55
  # The shortest thing the envelope can do: forty milliseconds, all of it
  # thrown at the filter. Each step is a click with a pitch rather than a
  # note with a filter on it, and a line of them is closer to a hi-hat
  # pattern than to a bass part.
  cutoff         250 Hz
  resonance      0.8
  envmod         1
  decay          45 ms
  accent         0.85
  slide          20 ms
  drive          0.18
  volume         0.92

patch Wasp family=dirty  note=48 range=36..67
  # The same idea carried up into the treble, where the resonance stops
  # sounding like a filter and starts sounding like a second oscillator
  # buzzing above the note.
  cutoff         1800 Hz
  resonance      0.88
  envmod         0.4
  decay          180 ms
  accent         0.55
  slide          40 ms
  drive          0.1
  volume         0.75

patch Crawl family=motion  note=24 range=24..48
  # Low, slow, and sliding most of the way: the line that moves underneath
  # everything else rather than driving it. A long decay on a low cutoff
  # means the filter is still opening when the bar ends.
  cutoff         120 Hz
  resonance      0.7
  envmod         0.85
  decay          1100 ms
  accent         0.4
  slide          300 ms
  drive          0.12
  volume         1.32

patch Talk family=motion  note=36 range=24..55
  # Envelope all the way up on a middling cutoff, so each note starts bright
  # and closes to a vowel. The most speech-like thing a one-pole-per-stage
  # ladder can do, and the reason people say these lines sing.
  cutoff         620 Hz
  resonance      0.6
  envmod         1
  decay          340 ms
  accent         0.7
  slide          70 ms
  drive          0.1
  volume         0.85

patch Siren family=motion  note=36 range=24..55
  # Longer than Long Sweep by half again, with the resonance up: the filter
  # takes nearly three seconds to travel and the line underneath it is
  # almost beside the point. Hold the pattern and listen to the sweep.
  cutoff         85 Hz
  resonance      0.9
  envmod         1
  decay          2600 ms
  accent         0.35
  slide          80 ms
  drive          0.14
  volume         0.93

# --- Beyond ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# The sub oscillator, the band-pass, the pulse width and the drive are
# four things the machine this descends from never had. This half is
# what they are for, and it is the half the machine is named after.

patch "Sub Hollow" family=sub  note=36 range=24..55
  # The sub is the note and the pulse is the edge on it. Narrow width, filter
  # low, very little envelope: a bass that stays where it is put.
  wave           #1
  pw             0.24
  sub            0.65
  cutoff         220 Hz
  resonance      0.38
  envmod         0.3
  decay          480 ms
  accent         0.5
  drive          0.08
  volume         0.91

# Stops at 45: above that the cutoff at the floor leaves the sub with the
# note only thirteen decibels behind it, and it is a sub no longer.
patch "Sub Drop" family=sub  note=36 range=24..45
  # All sub and no edge: the cutoff is at the floor, so what is left is the
  # octave below the note and almost nothing else. For the bottom of a mix
  # rather than the front of one.
  wave           #1
  pw             0.5
  sub            1
  cutoff         70 Hz
  resonance      0.2
  envmod         0.1
  decay          900 ms
  accent         0.3
  drive          0.0
  volume         1.46

patch "Band Squelch" family=band  note=36 range=24..55
  # Band-pass: no bottom and no top, so what is left is the resonance itself.
  # Volume at the top and drive doing the rest, and it is still the quietest
  # thing here by two decibels - which is what taking the ends off a spectrum
  # costs, not something to pretend away.
  mode           #1
  cutoff         690 Hz
  resonance      0.85
  envmod         0.8
  decay          120 ms
  accent         0.9
  drive          0.55
  volume         1.50

patch "Band Lead" family=band  note=48 range=36..67
  # Band-pass with the resonance near the top, played an octave up. The
  # machine does leave the bass register, and this is what it sounds like
  # when it does: a thin reedy lead with the filter as its tone.
  mode           #1
  cutoff         1150 Hz
  resonance      0.93
  envmod         0.45
  decay          200 ms
  accent         0.6
  slide          90 ms
  drive          0.35
  volume         1.26

patch "Thin Ice" family=sub  note=48 range=36..67
  # Right at the top of the resonance with almost no envelope: it whistles on
  # the note rather than under it. The one that shows resonance is a tone.
  cutoff         1400 Hz
  resonance      0.97
  envmod         0.15
  decay          220 ms
  accent         0.4
  drive          0.05
  volume         0.76

patch Buzzsaw family=dirty  note=36 range=24..55
  # Filter nearly out of the way and the drive doing the work. Not acid at
  # all - the machine's other end, and the reason drive is in the voice
  # rather than in an insert.
  cutoff         3200 Hz
  resonance      0.25
  envmod         0.25
  decay          400 ms
  accent         0.55
  drive          0.85
  volume         0.91

patch "Hollow Pulse" family=sub  note=36 range=24..55
  # The pulse squeezed almost shut. A narrow pulse is all odd harmonics and
  # no body, which the filter then has almost nothing to take away from: it
  # is nasal where everything above it is round, and it is a width the
  # machine this descends from could not set.
  wave           #1
  pw             0.08
  cutoff         700 Hz
  resonance      0.55
  envmod         0.5
  decay          220 ms
  accent         0.6
  slide          55 ms
  drive          0.12
  volume         1.06

patch Screamer family=dirty  note=36 range=24..55
  # Drive at the top with the resonance still up, which the original could
  # only do by putting a pedal after it. The filter is still doing its job
  # underneath; what comes out the other side is not clean and is not meant
  # to be.
  cutoff         620 Hz
  resonance      0.8
  envmod         0.75
  decay          240 ms
  accent         0.8
  slide          45 ms
  drive          1
  volume         0.87

patch Ghost family=band  note=48 range=36..67
  # Band-pass with the resonance low and the decay long: no bottom, no top
  # and no bite, so what is left is a breathy middle that sits under a track
  # rather than on it. The machine as a texture rather than a line.
  mode           #1
  # The band sits on the note rather than three octaves above it: at 900 Hz
  # a bass note's fundamental is nowhere near the pass band and the patch was
  # ten decibels under the bank instead of five.
  cutoff         380 Hz
  resonance      0.5
  envmod         0.25
  decay          1400 ms
  accent         0.3
  slide          180 ms
  drive          0.1
  volume         1.50

patch "Octave Down" family=motion  note=48 range=36..60
  # The tuning control put a whole octave down with the sub underneath it.
  # Play it where a lead would sit and it answers an octave and a half below
  # your hand, which is a thing a bass with one oscillator cannot do.
  tune           -12 st
  sub            0.55
  cutoff         300 Hz
  resonance      0.5
  envmod         0.45
  decay          420 ms
  accent         0.5
  slide          60 ms
  drive          0.1
  volume         1.06

# A fixed band on a moving note: below the tenor register the band sits
# thirty-five harmonics above the fundamental and there is no note left in
# it, so the range starts where the band can still reach.
patch Reed family=classic  note=48 range=36..67
  # The pulse at the far end from Hollow Pulse - nearly all the way open,
  # which is the same waveform inverted and sounds nothing like it once the
  # band-pass has taken the ends off. Thin, reedy, and the closest this
  # machine comes to being blown rather than plucked.
  wave           #1
  pw             0.9
  mode           #1
  cutoff         520 Hz
  resonance      0.7
  envmod         0.5
  decay          380 ms
  accent         0.55
  slide          90 ms
  drive          0.25
  volume         1.41

patch Grind family=dirty  note=36 range=24..48
  # Sub most of the way up, cutoff low, drive high. The distortion is working
  # on the octave below the note rather than on the note, which is what makes
  # it a texture instead of a fuzz pedal.
  # 0.4 rather than 0.8: at 0.8 the octave below was eighteen decibels
  # ahead of the note, so you played one note and heard another.
  sub            0.4
  cutoff         190 Hz
  resonance      0.4
  envmod         0.35
  decay          600 ms
  accent         0.6
  drive          0.9
  volume         0.98

# Same again, higher: a band at 2600 Hz has nothing to say about a note at
# 100.
patch Glass family=short  note=60 range=49..79
  # Band-pass with the resonance at the very top and almost no envelope:
  # the filter rings on its own and the oscillator is only there to start it.
  # Played high it is a ping rather than a bass at all.
  mode           #1
  cutoff         2600 Hz
  resonance      0.99
  envmod         0.08
  decay          260 ms
  accent         0.3
  slide          35 ms
  drive          0.05
  volume         0.62

patch Wide family=band  note=36 range=24..55
  # A square and the sub together with the filter well out of the way: no
  # acid in it at all, just a large simple bass with two octaves in it. The
  # one to reach for when the line is not the point.
  wave           #1
  pw             0.5
  sub            1
  cutoff         900 Hz
  resonance      0.15
  envmod         0.1
  decay          700 ms
  accent         0.4
  slide          50 ms
  drive          0.06
  volume         1.02

patch Rasp family=dirty  note=36 range=24..55
  # Mid-range, half driven, resonance only half up: the filter is shaping
  # distortion rather than the other way round. Sits where a guitar would.
  cutoff         1100 Hz
  resonance      0.5
  envmod         0.3
  decay          200 ms
  accent         0.6
  slide          45 ms
  drive          0.7
  volume         0.92

patch Pluck family=short  note=36 range=24..55
  # No resonance, a very short decay and a little drive: the filter closes
  # before it can ring, so what is left is the attack and a short woody body.
  # Round's opposite, and the only thing here that does not sound like a
  # filter at all.
  cutoff         1500 Hz
  resonance      0.05
  envmod         0.55
  decay          110 ms
  accent         0.5
  slide          30 ms
  drive          0.3
  volume         0.95
